            <h2>Travel Guide: About Charlottesville</h2>
            <p>Before starting your journey on the Charlottesville to Philadelphia train, spending some time exploring Charlottesville can make your trip even more rewarding. Charlottesville is not just a departure point - it's a destination filled with history, culture, and natural beauty. </p>
            <h3>About Charlottesville</h3>
            <p>Charlottesville is a historic city in central Virginia and home to the University of Virginia, founded by Thomas Jefferson in 1819. Nestled at the foothills of the Blue Ridge Mountains, it offers a rich tapestry of presidential history, academic excellence, and outdoor recreation. </p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Home to three U.S. presidents</b> - Thomas Jefferson, James Madison, and James Monroe</li>
                <li>A UNESCO World Heritage Site (Monticello and UVA's Academical Village)</li>
                <li>Famous for the Monticello Wine Trail, named 2023 Wine Region of the Year by Wine Enthusiast</li>
                <li>A vibrant arts and food scene centered around the historic Downtown Mall</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Whether you're visiting for a few hours or a few days, Charlottesville offers countless experiences before your onward journey. </p>
            <h3>Weather in Charlottesville</h3>
            <p>Charlottesville has a humid subtropical climate with four distinct seasons, meaning weather conditions change throughout the year. </p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Spring (Mar-May): </b> Mild temperatures in the 50s-70s, blooming dogwoods and azaleas, ideal for walking and winery visits</li>
                <li><b>Summer (Jun-Aug): </b> Warm, averaging 70s-80s°F, peak tourist season with university activities</li>
                <li><b>Autumn (Sep-Nov): </b> Cool and scenic with stunning fall foliage over the Blue Ridge Mountains, temperatures in the 50s-70s</li>
                <li><b>Winter (Dec-Feb): </b> Cold, averaging 30s-40s°F, occasional snow, quieter tourist season</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>What This Means for Travelers</h3>
            <p>Spring and autumn offer the most comfortable temperatures for walking and outdoor activities, with autumn being especially beautiful as the Blue Ridge Mountains turn orange and yellow. Summers are warm and lively, ideal for sightseeing but pack sunscreen. Winters can be cold but offer a quieter experience with fewer crowds. </p>
            <h3>Things to Do Before Departure</h3>
            <p>If you have time before boarding your train from Charlottesville to Philadelphia, here are a few ideas: </p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Explore nearby landmarks: </b> Visit Monticello or take a stroll through the University of Virginia's Academical Village</li>
